# Which bytes a checkout holds, the same on every system.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, so without this file a
# Windows checkout, the CI runner's included, writes every text file with CRLF
# line endings. The Rust suite does not mind: a clone made that way passed
# every test and drew every example figure byte for byte. The shell does.
# docs/data/draw.sh, which draws the site's figures, answers its `set -e`
# with "set: -: invalid option" and then tries to cd into a folder whose name
# ends in a carriage return. So text is LF in the index, which it already
# was, and LF in every working tree, whatever the local setting says. Adding
# this file renormalised no tracked file, and a clone made under autocrlf
# with it in place holds no CRLF anywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The figures are compared byte for byte with what the code draws, by the
# example job and by the playground's tests, so git never rewrites a line
# ending in one. Under text=auto a renderer that began to write a CR would
# have it quietly normalised away on commit, rather than showing as the
# difference it is. Every drawn figure is one line with no newline at all,
# and the hand-drawn logos keep the LF they were committed with.
*.svg -text

# Binary by name, not by git's guess at the content. The guess is right for
# every file here today, and a guess is not what should keep a BAM intact.
# The list runs ahead of the repository on purpose: it names the formats the
# readers and their fixtures deal in, the compressed and indexed ones, the
# binary ones the command line names and refuses, the playground's wasm and
# the pictures and documents a page may one day carry.
*.bam binary
*.bai binary
*.gz binary
*.tbi binary
*.csi binary
*.zip binary
*.bw binary
*.bb binary
*.2bit binary
*.bcf binary
*.hic binary
*.cool binary
*.mcool binary
*.wasm binary
*.png binary
*.pdf binary
